{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Sleepwalker","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"description":"A mythic science-fiction story of seven children, an impossible book and ruins on distant worlds that answer their dreams.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://sleepwalker.in/about/"}],"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-22T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A reader sits at a window as the whole city and its circular tower emerge beyond it.\nA reader sits at a window as the whole city and its circular tower emerge beyond it.\nSleepwalker\nA book opens. Far beyond waking, the ruins answer.\nBook\nDream\nReturn\nBeyond waking\nScroll\nSeven children, one impossible book.\nDistant ruins, calling them back.\nA seated figure in orange faces a tall mechanical figure in a luminous vaulted room.\nA seated figure in orange faces a tall mechanical figure in a luminous vaulted room.\nThe dreams reach beyond sleep.\nTheir dreams carry details of places they have never seen. The ruins begin to stir.\nOther worlds\nTall architectural forms rise among trees and dense foliage in green mist.\nThe world they inherit\nThe future keeps its records, yet much of its past is unreadable. Names and rituals survive what they once meant.\nDense urban façades and signs glow red and turquoise through haze.\nGrowing up between worlds\nThe children have to live with what they see.\nYears later\nA red-scarfed observer stands in a stone forecourt facing the city, its circular tower and water channels.\nA red-scarfed observer stands in a stone forecourt facing the city, its circular tower and water channels.\nThe dreams are not finished.\nThe book is lost. Years later, the ruins draw them back together.\nWould you want to remember?\nSeven lives, scattered by the same impossible childhood.\nA graphic novel in the making by Harsh Joshi, with an inquiry into its Vedic world and the methods of making it.\nAbout Sleepwalker\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/opening/landscape/still.webp","description":"A reader sits at a window as the whole city and its circular tower emerge beyond it."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/opening/portrait/still.webp","description":"A reader sits at a window as the whole city and its circular tower emerge beyond it."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/encounter/landscape/still.webp","description":"A seated figure in orange faces a tall mechanical figure in a luminous vaulted room."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/encounter/portrait/still.webp","description":"A seated figure in orange faces a tall mechanical figure in a luminous vaulted room."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/forest/landscape/still.webp","description":"Tall architectural forms rise among trees and dense foliage in green mist."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/city/landscape/still.webp","description":"Dense urban façades and signs glow red and turquoise through haze."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/coda/landscape/still.webp","description":"A red-scarfed observer stands in a stone forecourt facing the city, its circular tower and water channels."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/assets/content/sleepwalker/media/painted-scenes/coda/portrait/still.webp","description":"A red-scarfed observer stands in a stone forecourt facing the city, its circular tower and water channels."}],"url":"https://sleepwalker.in/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"},"about":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#entity","identifier":"project:sleepwalker-graphic-novel","@type":"Book","name":"Sleepwalker","description":"A mythic science-fiction story of seven children, an impossible book and ruins on distant worlds that answer their dreams.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand"},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Sleepwalker","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/"}]}]},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#website","@type":"WebSite","name":"Sleepwalker","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"},"about":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#entity"}],"usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"}},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:sleepwalker","name":"Sleepwalker","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#project","@type":"Project","identifier":"project:sleepwalker","name":"Sleepwalker"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#entity","@type":"Book","identifier":"project:sleepwalker-graphic-novel","name":"Sleepwalker"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:causality","name":"Causality","url":"https://causality.in/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://causality.in/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:causality","name":"Causality"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon"}]},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:post-agi-economics","name":"Post-AGI Economics","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","name":"Post-AGI Economics"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","@type":"CreativeWork","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","name":"One Yardstick"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:rawthoughts","name":"RawThoughts","description":"The applied engineering, consulting and publishing brand of RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited.","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:scope","name":"Scope"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:counsel","name":"Counsel"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:gazette","name":"Gazette"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/weave/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:weave","name":"Weave"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/rawfish/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:rawfish","name":"RawFish"}]},{"@id":"https://reve.fashion/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:reve","name":"Rêve","http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://reve.fashion/#project","@type":"Project","identifier":"project:reve","name":"Rêve"}},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}],"brand":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://reve.fashion/#brand"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/about/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-about","@type":"AboutPage","name":"About Sleepwalker","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"description":"A mythic science-fiction graphic novel by Harsh Joshi, with an inquiry into its Vedic world and methods for making it.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}],"text":"Seven children encounter an impossible book. Years later, with the book lost and their lives scattered, dreams of ruins on distant worlds draw them back together.\nSleepwalker is a mythic science-fiction graphic novel. Its questions about time, memory and consciousness also find room in an inquiry and in methods of making: reading a passage closely, following what a witness can know, or discovering how a page turn changes a scene.\nHarsh Joshi is developing the story and its world. Sleepwalker is a project of RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited (RTEPL).\nReading the Vedic world closely\nA familiar figure can enter a different relationship in another account. A passage can resist the meaning a reader hoped to borrow from it. Following those differences opens questions about time, attention, consciousness and devotion.\nRead Markandeya’s encounters across adjacent chapters, or follow a sound towards silence. Particular accounts, translations and interpretations give the inquiry its substance.\nThe Vedic-world inquiry\nMaking the story into a page\nA character must remain recognisable when the view changes. Moving a speech balloon can change who witnessed an event. A page turn can reveal a relationship that no individual image contained.\nThe creative methods work through these choices in image and sequence and lettering. Generative workflows make alternatives easier to explore; composition, controlled refinement and reading at the intended size give those alternatives a deliberate form.\nCreative methods\nDifferent ways into the same questions\nThe story lets a reader encounter a world before being given an explanation for it. The essays of the Vedic-world inquiry can stay with a passage or a question, while the creative methods examine the decisions that give an encounter its form. Each can be read independently. A traditional account may inform a scene without becoming its script; a method may suggest a technique without becoming a finished page. These crossings are part of how Sleepwalker is taking shape.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/about/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#entity","identifier":"project:sleepwalker-graphic-novel","@type":"Book","name":"Sleepwalker","description":"A mythic science-fiction story of seven children, an impossible book and ruins on distant worlds that answer their dreams.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand"},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Sleepwalker","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-essays","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Blog","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-information-architecture-for-a-story","name":"The several orders of a story"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-scale-is-not-a-measure-of-consciousness","name":"The world inside a breath"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Standalone essays on cosmic scale and the craft of making a story.","text":"","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-information-architecture-for-a-story","@type":"Article","name":"The several orders of a story","headline":"The several orders of a story","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["story chronology","reader discovery","character belief","revision dependencies","information architecture"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The several orders of a story","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/"]},"description":"One bell rings at noon; changing the event, a witness's belief or the reader's discovery leads to three different stories.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2025-07-14","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The several orders of a story\nA story has more than one order. There is the order in which things happen, the order in which its characters understand them, and the order in which the reader finds out. A mystery depends on their difference: an event can be over before anyone understands it, and a reader can suspect something that a character has yet to see. That gap is something I can write with.\nThe bell rang once\nImagine a bell ringing at noon. One witness hears it, another sees a stopped clock, and a third arrives later. The narrative shows the clock first, introduces the bell later, and only then lets us hear the first witness's account. Moving that account earlier would change the reader's experience without changing when the bell rang; changing a witness's belief might change their next action while leaving the bell untouched. “Move this earlier” might mean moving the event, a memory of it, or its appearance on the page. Each sends me back to different scenes.\nThree sequences trace the bell, the witnesses and the reader, contrasting a change to the event with a change to its telling.\nThe arrows show sequence, not elapsed time or a shared clock.\nSuppose the first witness leaves at 12:15. Moving the bell from noon to 13:00 would remove their chance to hear it. Showing their account earlier changes what the reader knows while leaving those event times intact. The Notes follow the scenes each revision would affect and the choices it leaves me.\nThe clock's face is an observation, not a reliable timestamp for the bell. Its place in the reader's sequence can suggest a connection before the story gives enough information to judge it.\nA clue is a relationship, not only an object\nThe stopped clock might establish a location, suggest a time, or mislead someone who assumes it is running. If I change where it hangs, the witness's opportunity to see it may disappear. If I change when the reader sees it, the opportunity remains but the inference changes. The clue lives in these relations, not only in the inventory of objects. A later scene may depend on the clock having been seen, on a mistaken conclusion about it, or on the reader remembering its face.\nInformation architecture helps when it makes those dependencies legible. Instead of rewriting every scene that mentions a clock, I can follow the opportunity, belief and action that the revised premise affected. Where a dependency is uncertain, the uncertainty is itself useful: it exposes a creative choice that still needs an answer.\nBelief is not the whole state\nContinue the bell example. Suppose the late arrival is frightened when the two witnesses meet later, and the first witness offers support without explaining the event. Trust between them might improve while neither person's knowledge of the bell's cause changes. If a model of the story folds trust and confidence about causes into one score, support becomes evidence by accident. Keeping those states distinct allows a person to feel safer with somebody while still doubting their account.\nAn institution introduces another path. It receives a genuine account of the bell, but without the stopped clock that would have made it doubt the time. Its staff can become more confident and choose the wrong intervention without anybody inventing a false observation. Restoring the clock to the account changes that inference and the decisions based on it. It need not erase the trust that grew between the witnesses, or undo the consequences of the intervention already made. State changes travel through their particular relationships; one correction is not a reset of the world.\nThe drawings can change too. A remembered scene might lose colour or break into fragments as a witness begins to doubt their interpretation. The bell still rang at noon. Fear might change how the witness is framed without moving the clock. The image needs to let us distinguish whose experience changed and what they are remembering; otherwise a changed palette can look like a changed event.\nIn a model that keeps them apart, actions leave something behind in people, relationships and institutions. Accounts can be corrected without erasing consequences, and the same occurrence can be encountered in several forms. The rules are choices about a story, not measurements of human psychology. They are useful if they show me a consequence I would otherwise miss.\nWhen a reading changes the invention\nA traditional passage can change what I want an invented rule to do. Following a symbol through its transformations may make its fictional role seem too static, or reveal a consequence I had missed. I might revise that role or keep it as an independent invention. The relation is useful because the reading can push back on the work: an earlier image need not dictate what the passage means, and a passage need not dictate the only story I can make.\nFollowing a change through the story\nThe story model I am developing for Sleepwalker keeps track of its people and objects, their changing states, the order of scenes, transformations and the images through which we encounter them. An altered observation might unsettle a character's next choice, while an illustration might expose a relation that the prose left vague. Following those changes can send the work in a new direction.\nI still have to decide what to do with what the model shows me. Eventually the reader encounters none of this machinery, only the space between panels, a remembered object, an account that arrives late. The several orders become worthwhile when their arrangement lets that reader discover the relation for themselves.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/bell-orders.svg","description":"Three sequences trace the bell, the witnesses and the reader, contrasting a change to the event with a change to its telling.","caption":"The arrows show sequence, not elapsed time or a shared clock.\n"}],"url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-scale-is-not-a-measure-of-consciousness","@type":"Article","name":"The world inside a breath","headline":"The world inside a breath","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["Breath of Cosmos","cosmic time","infinity and limits","Vishnu's breath","Markandeya","breath and consciousness"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The world inside a breath","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/"]},"description":"An unbounded cosmic breath and a finite human interval open a creative question: how does a witness encounter a world larger than their own frame?","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-02-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The world inside a breath\nA breath gives an immense world somewhere intimate to begin. Attend to its beginning and end, then imagine an entire life passing within it. From inside that life there were mornings, work, waiting and people who mattered. From the other frame, barely a moment. What would let a reader encounter both scales without losing the person inside the smaller one?\nThis is one of the questions behind Sleepwalker. In Breath of Cosmos, I begin with a breathing pattern as an input to an imagined model of consciousness, then extend the comparison into cosmic time. The argument makes a definite choice: the cosmic duration is unbounded, while the human breath remains a finite, nonzero interval. Its ratio is infinite. Here I want to follow what that construction makes possible for a story and an image.\nThe interval stays small; the frame keeps opening\nTake the essay's illustrative human breath of seven seconds. Let B be the cosmic duration, expressed in seconds. Then the two directions of the comparison are\n\n\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\frac{B}{7}=\\infty,\n\\qquad\n\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\frac{7}{B}=0.\n\nThe human interval has not become zero. Its proportion tends to zero as the other duration grows without bound. This matters to the imaginative movement: a life does not need to disappear for the frame containing it to become immeasurably larger.\nThe essay uses breath and time as proxies in a thought experiment about consciousness. Its further possibility is a cosmic or collective consciousness containing individual forms of experience. The limit expresses the scale relation; the interpretation asks what it might mean to belong to that whole. A story gives that question a witness. The witness has a particular history, even when the world exceeds the history's familiar terms.\nA calendar can lead the imagination towards the opening\nVedic cosmological accounts give the reader several changes of scale to encounter. In the Bhaktivedanta edition of the Bhāgavata Purāṇa, 3.11.18 gives a four-yuga cycle whose commentary expresses it as 4.32 million human years. 3.11.22 places a thousand such cycles within Brahmā's day, followed by a night of equal duration. 3.11.38 then compares the two halves of Brahmā's life with a moment for the Supreme. A lifetime on one scale becomes a brief interval on another.\nEach specified period is finite, however immense it feels beside a human life. The Brahma-saṁhitā, 5.48, supplies the image of the Brahmās' lives within Mahā-Vishnu's breath. Breath of Cosmos takes that image further: suppose there is no final bound to the duration we are comparing with our own.\nThe notes calculate how many seven-second intervals fit within one of those finite periods. Here I want to stay with the next step. What happens to the world we thought we knew when the frame around it can keep opening?\nThe place the witness knows\nIn Markandeya's encounter, the impossible scale arrives through an ordinary recognition. The witness finds a world inside the child's body, then recognises his own hermitage. The familiar remains there after the frame has become impossible. That recognition makes the encounter more unsettling than a succession of larger containers would have been.\nI can imagine drawing the change as a series of receding views: a person, a landscape, a world, another world around it. The figure becomes a marker of size. But what if I keep the reader close to the figure? A remembered path could remain recognisable while its surroundings cease to fit the account. A detail that seemed reassuring could become the very thing that makes the new world impossible to dismiss.\nThe two compositions offer different experiences of the same ambition. One tells the reader how large the world is. The other lets them discover that the world they know has acquired a place within something they cannot yet understand. The second gives the person more to do than demonstrate scale.\nWhat continues between the frames?\nA graphic novel can make that discovery happen through sequence. A place first encountered as ordinary can return with a changed relation to the world around it. The reader carries the earlier encounter across the interval between panels. If every detail changes, recognition becomes difficult; if nothing changes, the expansion may never become an encounter at all.\nThat brings the cosmic question into the craft of keeping something recognisable through a sequence. Which feature must survive? Where should the reader first notice the difference? What can the witness recognise before they can explain it? These are decisions an author can work on without asking the picture to prove a theory of consciousness.\nA small experience can be contained within a larger account and still be the experience through which that account becomes available. The breath supplies a rhythm. The construction opens the scale. The witness brings a world into the encounter, and the story has to let us feel what happens when that world is no longer the whole.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-collections","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Thoughts","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-vedic-world-inquiry","name":"The Vedic-world inquiry"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-creative-methods","name":"Creative methods"}}]},"description":"Two connected inquiries: the Vedic world and the methods of creative expression.","text":"","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-creative-methods","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Creative methods","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"description":"How visual intention, generative tools and structured continuity can support deliberate making.","text":"A figure at a threshold can be smaller than the opening and still hold the centre of a composition. Moving the figure, widening the frame or changing the contrast alters that relationship. The useful discovery is more particular than “this looks good”: something in the arrangement makes the image carry an idea.\nIn Sleepwalker's Home, the closing view puts a red-scarfed figure on the approach to a circular tower. The city has scale, but the small person facing it gives me a way into that scale. An image is a beginning starts with that frame and asks what would need to survive a revision.\nGenerative tools make variations available quickly. The craft is in recognising the choice worth developing and carrying it into another frame. Pose and expression need to change without erasing identity; a new view needs to belong to the same space. Controlled refinement can preserve selected relationships while leaving others open to invention. The progression from frame to sequence takes that problem into a page someone else can read.\nContinuity reaches beyond appearance. An event can happen before a character understands it, and a reader can see its consequence before its cause. Those different orders of a story let a revelation change the significance of an earlier panel without changing what happened there. Even the position of a few words can change whose account the reader receives. Structure makes those relationships workable; composition and pacing give them an experience.\nA variation can reveal something worth keeping. Put two good images next to each other, though, and they may tell a story neither told alone. That is where making the page starts to change the idea.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-words-and-space","@type":"Article","name":"Words and the space around them","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/"},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-from-frame-to-sequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From frame to sequence","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-from-frame-to-sequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From frame to sequence","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Creative methods","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From frame to sequence","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}}]}],"description":"Carry a visual choice through character continuity into the reader's inference between panels.","text":"A compelling frame offers a relationship worth keeping: a figure's scale, an opening around it, a line of light. Following it into another image asks which parts can vary while the person and place remain recognisable. Following those images onto a page asks what the reader can infer between them.\nThat progression changes the unit of the work. The individual image becomes a continuous presence, then a sequence whose meaning exceeds any one frame. What began as a visual choice becomes an experience someone else completes.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-creative-methods","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Creative methods","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-prompt-is-not-a-production-method","@type":"Article","name":"An image is a beginning","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-continuity-is-more-than-a-seed","@type":"Article","name":"Keeping a character through a sequence","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-the-gutter-is-an-inference","@type":"Article","name":"What happens between the panels","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-prompt-is-not-a-production-method","@type":"Article","name":"An image is a beginning","headline":"An image is a beginning","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["generative image composition","human scale","ComfyUI refinement","ControlNet conditioning","masked paint-over"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","@type":"WebPage","name":"An image is a beginning","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Creative methods","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From frame to sequence","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"An image is a beginning","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/"]},"description":"A generated frame reveals a relation worth keeping. Composition, spatial conditioning and refinement give that discovery a way to develop.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-05","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"An image is a beginning\nA generated frame can show me something I did not know how to ask for. That is part of its appeal: the image arrives before the intention is fully verbal, and choosing what to keep can become a way of discovering the intention. Carrying the discovery into another frame asks a harder question. What, exactly, was worth keeping?\nThe part that mattered\nA small figure before an enormous threshold can hold the relationship I want even when its materials are wrong. Another image can have exquisite surfaces and lose the relationship entirely. If I judge both by how impressive they look in isolation, I have no reliable way to choose between them.\nSleepwalker's visual direction begins with the human anchor. The environment carries scale, but the person gives that scale a reason to be felt. A cool field and warm focal light can guide attention towards that relation; weathered surfaces can make the environment feel like something encountered rather than freshly invented. A return path or an object someone has maintained can suggest a world inhabited beyond its spectacle. Those treatments work when the composition already holds the person and the threshold together. The prompt becomes a way to discover which decision should survive the next attempt, rather than a request to produce the whole work at once.\nA red-scarfed figure stands on a stone forecourt, facing a distant circular tower across water channels; the dark figure interrupts the warm central axis of the city.\nThe closing composition from Sleepwalker's Home.\nThe figure stands directly on the approach to the circular tower. Its dark silhouette interrupts the warm central line, while the scarf gives the nearby body a small movement against the enormous, nearly symmetrical setting. The city could remain impressive without the figure; with it, the image asks where someone is standing and what they are facing. That is a relation I can point to and try to preserve.\nOne prompt cannot hold every decision equally\nComposition, character identity, expression, environment and lettering are different kinds of work. Asking one generation to solve them together makes it difficult to improve one without disturbing the others. The route I intend to explore begins with frames I direct in Midjourney, followed by local ComfyUI and controlled refinement that separates those decisions. A composition can remain fixed while a character's treatment changes; a pose can be specified separately from the appearance of the person holding it. Lettering can be composed for the page rather than left to chance in an image.\nSeparating those decisions lets each change become more legible. If a revised pose turns inquiry into confrontation, I can see what the pose did without also changing the environment. If the colour treatment moves the emotional centre, I can reconsider that treatment while retaining the staging. Controlled work makes room for discovery because fewer unrelated choices are lost with each attempt.\nMaking a change without losing the frame\nA workable study can start in the least polished form: a thumbnail fixing the small figure, the opening and the masses beyond it. At that scale, a stronger silhouette can do more than a more intricate building. I would carry the chosen composition into a pose or depth condition, develop the person's identity separately, and then work on the environment around the person. Masks make the separation practical: an expression can change inside one region while the important contour and light direction outside it remain deliberate choices.\nSpatial conditioning is useful here because it holds a different sort of information from a text prompt. The ControlNet paper develops conditions including edges, depth and human pose. A depth map can help preserve the threshold's spatial relation while a pose condition gives the person a specific action. Neither names the emotional proposition of the scene. That remains in the staging: whether the shoulders yield, whether the head looks into the space, and how much distance the composition leaves before entry.\nEnvironment, lettering and final paint-over then answer their own questions. A ruin can carry mass and silence without an indiscriminate layer of luminous glyphs. Lettering needs space before the surface detail fills the page. Paint-over can recover a hand, a face or a material boundary that the generation made plausible but narratively wrong. These are not failures to automate enough. They are places where the work needs a decision finer than “make another image”.\nFor the threshold study, the stages would carry different decisions forward:\nStage\tDecision worth retaining\tWhat can still change\t\nThumbnail\tSmall figure, opening and large surrounding masses\tMaterials, detail and expression\t\nSpatial conditioning\tPose and depth relationship\tSurface treatment within that geometry\t\nCharacter and environment passes\tRecognisable person and the light connecting them to the threshold\tLocal corrections inside selected masks\t\nLettering and paint-over\tReading order, gesture and emotional focus\tDetail that competes with the sequence\t\nKeeping the conditioning inputs and model choices makes an attempt repeatable enough to investigate, but the thumbnail remains an important check against false progress. A beautiful render that hides the witness behind ornament has lost the composition's point. A rougher one that preserves the relation may be the better foundation. The sequence must retain the decision the frame helped me discover.\nThe image can disagree with the idea\nA drawing may reveal that a scene's supposed emotional centre is somewhere else, or expose a weakness in the written premise. I want that disagreement to be possible: the image can send a question back to the work, and I can choose whether to follow it. The same openness matters in reading a tradition, where a source can resist what I hoped to make from it. When a frame does reveal a compelling relation, keeping the character through a small sequence lets me follow how that relation changes. What survives is a choice I can understand and develop, rather than a look I can only try to repeat.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/forecourt.webp","description":"A red-scarfed figure stands on a stone forecourt, facing a distant circular tower across water channels; the dark figure interrupts the warm central axis of the city.","caption":"The closing composition from Sleepwalker's Home.\n"}],"url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-from-frame-to-sequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From frame to sequence","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-continuity-is-more-than-a-seed","@type":"Article","name":"Keeping a character through a sequence","headline":"Keeping a character through a sequence","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["character continuity","visual registers","spatial conditioning","dream logic","viewing size"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Keeping a character through a sequence","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Creative methods","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From frame to sequence","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Keeping a character through a sequence","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/"]},"description":"A person approaches a doorway through changing views and material registers; recognition makes the variation expressive.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-07","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Keeping a character through a sequence\nThe reader sees a change before they know why it happened. In a comic, that is powerful. In generated art, it can also be an accident. If the face, clothing or position of a carried object changes between two panels, the reader may take it as an event. A production inconsistency has acquired narrative meaning before anyone intended it to.\nAn early extraction for Sleepwalker's website made this problem concrete. The request was to separate a standing figure from its forest background. In the source, the figure's chest faced the viewer, with the head turned slightly to one side; the first extraction showed the figure's back. A change meant to remove scenery had changed the encounter: facing the reader and turning away invite different readings of attention and intention. The corrective request specified the original facing direction, shoulder position, lowered hands, proportions, clothing and bare feet. It asked for the person to remain the same while the background disappeared. The important constraint was the relation the pose carried, not simply a recognisable face.\nRecognition has more than one input\nConsider a neutral sequence: a person notices a doorway, approaches it and turns back. Expression and pose change. The silhouette, proportions and clothing need enough continuity for the person to remain recognisable. The doorway moves within the frame as the viewpoint changes, but its relation to the room must still make sense.\nA continuity sheet describes the person across views; a pose guide describes the action; a composition sketch describes where that action sits. A seed records one generation input rather than defining any of these relationships. The ControlNet paper describes spatial conditioning through inputs such as edges, depth and pose, giving the process a way to hold some spatial relations more deliberately. Character recognition still depends on which features remain legible through the changed view, and narrative meaning depends on what the sequence does with them.\nA different image can still be the same event\nSleepwalker's visual direction allows the same event to change with viewpoint, memory or emotional state. A recovered memory can have a different material treatment from a lived event while retaining the person, room and significant gesture. Recognition carries the relation across the change; the altered treatment lets the reader experience another way of approaching it.\nThe stable relation gives the variation something to disturb. A repeated composition with one changed detail can focus attention on that detail. If every feature changes at once, the reader may lose the very connection through which the variation was meant to speak. Continuity therefore creates expressive range rather than requiring every frame to look identical.\nThe register changes for a reason\nThe doorway study can move through several visual registers without changing its event. In a lived view, clear silhouettes and a cool illuminated field keep the person near. In recovered memory, amber and worn print can change the material distance while retaining the doorway and the gesture. A dream might begin with those same particulars and break only one relation: the opening grows beyond the wall that contains it, while the hand and the room remain recognisable. The impossible scale becomes legible because the rest of the image continues to honour the ordinary setting.\nThat single break is more useful than unrestricted surrealism. If the face, room, pose and viewpoint all drift, the reader cannot locate what became impossible. A maskable transformation also gives production a tractable question: preserve the agreed identity and geometry, then apply the intended break to the chosen relation. Dream logic has become a compositional operation rather than a prompt for strangeness.\nFor a study in corrupted attribution, I could repeat two nearly symmetric panels and alter just a shadow or the direction of a speech-balloon tail. The observation remains familiar while the reader's account of who acted becomes uncertain. The altered detail has to survive the intended viewing size; a clue that only exists at production resolution cannot do the same work on a phone. The change gives uncertainty a visible location in an otherwise familiar composition.\nResolution need not erase the difference. Two viewpoints could retain distinct palettes on one spread while their shared spatial relation finally aligns. The views become intelligible together, not interchangeable. In that possibility, continuity preserves both recognition and plurality: the same encounter can be followed through more than one way of seeing it.\nRegister\tRelation retained\tDeliberate variation\t\nLived event\tPerson, doorway and action\tClear silhouettes in a cool illuminated field\t\nRecovered memory\tRecognisable place and gesture\tAmber, worn-print material distance\t\nDream\tHand and ordinary room\tOpening grows beyond its containing wall\t\nCorrupted attribution\tFamiliar observation\tA shadow or balloon tail changes who seems responsible\t\nResolution\tShared spatial relation\tDistinct viewpoints align without losing their palettes\t\nThe record explains an attempt, not its meaning\nKeeping the workflow, models, conditioning inputs and parameters makes a changed result easier to inspect. Judging the sequence requires another scale of attention. A consistent face may draw attention away from the action; a speech balloon may cover the gesture that connects two panels; a page legible at full size may lose the relation on a phone. The production record explains an attempt, while thumbnails, lettering and the intended viewing size expose what that attempt lets a reader see.\nRecognition lets a change become meaningful: the reader sees that this is the person they knew, now turning away, returning or noticing something. Between the panels, they supply what the images leave out. Continuity gives that participation a stable world to act upon.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-from-frame-to-sequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From frame to sequence","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-the-gutter-is-an-inference","@type":"Article","name":"What happens between the panels","headline":"What happens between the panels","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["the gutter","comics closure","panel transitions","page-turn revelation","reader inference","visual pacing"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What happens between the panels","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Creative methods","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From frame to sequence","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What happens between the panels","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/","https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/"]},"description":"A door opens between two images. Panel order, withheld action and the physical page shape the relation the reader supplies.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-09","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What happens between the panels\nA hand approaches a closed door. In the next panel, a person stands inside the room. The door opened somewhere between the images, although nobody drew it opening. The reader made that part of the story happen, connecting the two visible moments with an action the page left out.\nChange the second panel to the person still outside, facing a locked door, and the relationship changes. Add somebody else holding a key and another explanation becomes possible. The images do not need a caption announcing each inference. Their arrangement gives the reader something to do.\nA person reaches towards a closed door; in the next panel, that person stands inside the room.\nA: the next frame supplies a consequence. Outside → inside.\nA person reaches towards a closed door; in the next panel, they remain outside beside another person holding a key.\nB: the next frame opens another possibility. Outside → outside, with someone holding a key.\nThe first frame is the same. The different second frames invite different accounts of the action left between them.\nThe strongest image may be wrong for the page\nA beautiful establishing frame can be redundant if the previous panel has already established the place. A spectacular interior can expose what a later page needs to withhold. A panel belongs to an action, a rhythm and an order of discovery: it may introduce the situation, sustain an unresolved moment, carry its turn or leave room for the consequence. A quieter frame can do more for the page if it lets the reader recognise what has changed.\nThe missing action belongs to the mystery\nA later revelation can revise the explanation of what the reader saw without replacing the observation. The door was closed; perhaps someone else opened it. The first explanation was incomplete, not necessarily the image. That possibility depends on ordinary continuity. If a handle changes sides because generation drifted, it may look like a clue; repeated accidents make it hard to know which discrepancies deserve attention. Keeping continuity through a sequence gives the reader a baseline against which an intentional discrepancy can matter.\nAttention changes the interval\nA sequence can sustain the doorway without repeatedly establishing it. One panel gives the place, another the hand approaching, a narrow interval holds the hesitation, and the next carries the irreversible discovery. A release panel can then show what the discovery did to the person. Changing the space given to hesitation changes how long the reader inhabits it, even when the imagined event lasts no longer. Moment-to-moment transitions attend to small changes; aspect-to-aspect views can gather a place through several details before its full relation becomes apparent.\nWords also take time to cross a page. A line can reach us before its speaker appears, carrying a voice through the interval. Where that voice belongs depends on the lettering and the space around it, another part of the composition rather than a caption explaining what the images already show.\nThe page has a body\nA reveal placed after a page turn arrives differently from the same reveal visible on the current spread. A phone's scroll is another physical arrangement: the words and images may be unchanged while the timing is not. I am interested in that material part of reading, the distance between frames, the density of a page, the room a silence receives. A dream sequence can alter one of those relations and let the disturbance travel through an otherwise recognisable page.\nWhat the page leaves out can become part of what it expresses. The event, the character's account and the reader's discovery meet in that space, with the reader supplying a relation rather than receiving its explanation. The door can stay unchanged on the page while becoming a different door in the reader's memory.\nScott McCloud's Understanding Comics (1993) calls the space between panels the gutter and the reader's work across it closure; moment-to-moment and aspect-to-aspect are two of the six transitions he names. The release panel is one of Neil Cohn's narrative categories in The Visual Language of Comics (2013).\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/doorway-consequence.svg","description":"A person reaches towards a closed door; in the next panel, that person stands inside the room.","caption":"A: the next frame supplies a consequence. Outside → inside.\n"},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/doorway-possibility.svg","description":"A person reaches towards a closed door; in the next panel, they remain outside beside another person holding a key.","caption":"B: the next frame opens another possibility. Outside → outside, with someone holding a key.\n"}],"url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-from-frame-to-sequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From frame to sequence","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-words-and-space","@type":"Article","name":"Words and the space around them","headline":"Words and the space around them","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["comics lettering","speech attribution","negative space","invented glyphs","visual accessibility"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Words and the space around them","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Creative methods","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Words and the space around them","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/"]},"description":"The same sentence can name a different witness when its balloon moves; an invented mark can acquire meaning in the interval between panels.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-10","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Words and the space around them\nPut “I saw it” beside two figures. A balloon pointing to the nearer figure makes one person the witness; a tail reaching towards the other changes who claims to have seen. Put the words in an established memory caption instead, and the moment of speaking may lie elsewhere. The sentence has not changed. Its place on the page has changed the account it gives us.\nThat makes lettering part of the story's evidence. If a later panel reveals that the apparent speaker was repeating somebody else's account, the first arrangement must have left room for that discovery. An arbitrary tail or a decorative change of type would spend the reader's attention on a relation the story never meant to offer. Ambiguity can be deliberate, but it needs a relationship worth being uncertain about.\nA voice reaches the page\nA line of speech can reach across a boundary before its speaker is visible. The reader encounters a voice, then discovers where it came from. Detached text can suggest another register only if its typography and position give a reason to read it differently. I want that difference to survive beyond colour: a person's speech, a machine translation and a remembered voice should remain distinguishable through shape, spacing and consistent conventions.\nThe convention earns trust through ordinary use. Once a reader knows how a remembered voice looks, a departure can become consequential. If every line arrives in a newly invented treatment, the departure has no baseline. The page asks the reader to learn a new code each time instead of following what the voice says.\nLettering also makes a route through a panel. Two balloons can be legible separately and still leave their order uncertain. Moving a line to make room for an image can change who seems to answer whom. I would review the exchange at the size it will actually be read, including a phone: the words, their order and their relation to the figures need to hold together. Enlarging a beautiful frame does not repair an exchange that cannot be followed.\nA mark can extend beyond its pieces\nNegative space can hold an unfinished relation rather than merely make a page elegant. Consider fragments of an invented glyph placed in successive panels. Their alignment could leave a shape in the space between them that no individual panel contains. Moving the fragments would alter that shape without changing the fragments themselves. This is one way an invented script could carry meaning in an arrangement, not only in its separate marks.\nThe gap needs to do something visible. If the pieces form the same complete mark inside each panel, their repetition only decorates the sequence. If one fragment becomes readable through its relation to another, moving or closing the interval changes the reading. A narrow screen might also separate the fragments and remove that relation. The composition would then need another treatment, not merely smaller lettering.\nThe action left between panels asks a reader to supply what is missing. Words and marks can use that space differently: locating a speaker, carrying a memory or completing an invented sign. Empty space becomes articulate when changing it changes what can be read.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/words-and-the-space-around-them/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-creative-methods","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Creative methods","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-vedic-world-inquiry","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"description":"Markandeya's changing encounters and the movement of inward sound open questions about scale, attention and devotion.","text":"What does Markandeya recognise inside a world contained by a child, and after that impossible encounter, what does he ask for? The passage turns somewhere between the two. I want to follow that turn, including the part that a retelling centred on cosmic spectacle might leave behind.\nThese readings begin with questions about time, consciousness and cause, then follow what the particular account says. In the Bhāgavata, one encounter with the cosmic child continues into another. In the Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā, attention follows sound towards silence. The names and images can feel familiar before their movement is. Reading the passage, and knowing which translation I am reading, gives that familiarity something to answer to.\nA story or an image may grow from the encounter. Sometimes the reading simply changes the question. The essays here leave room for both.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-symbol-is-not-a-dictionary-entry","@type":"Article","name":"Following a symbol through its transformations","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/"},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-markandeya-encounters","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-symbol-is-not-a-dictionary-entry","@type":"Article","name":"Following a symbol through its transformations","headline":"Following a symbol through its transformations","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["nada","Hatha Yoga Pradipika","sound and absorption","symbolic transformation"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Following a symbol through its transformations","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Following a symbol through its transformations","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/"]},"description":"Following inward sound towards silence reveals a movement of attention that an isolated definition of a symbol would miss.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-04-22","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Following a symbol through its transformations\nA sound grows quieter, and attention grows more absorbed. That movement interests me more than a symbol made louder until its meaning is impossible to miss. In the nāda passages of the Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā, what attention follows changes as the account develops. Reading the movement gives me something a dictionary entry cannot: a relation between a form, the attention it gathers and what happens when the form no longer holds the foreground.\nA sound followed into silence\nI am reading Svātmārāma through Pancham Sinh's 1914 English translation. In chapter IV, Sanskrit-numbered verses 84–86 move from loud, varied sounds through drums and bells towards finer comparisons such as flute, vīṇā and bee. Later, Sanskrit-numbered 101–102 associate the disappearance of sound with the supreme culmination and distinguish heard nāda as Shakti from formless Parameshvara. These are movements within the text's yogic and theological account, not an acoustic mechanism.\nThe middle of the account complicates a simple fading sound. Sanskrit-numbered verses 87–89 describe attention to subtler sound even amid loud sound, movement between coarse and subtle, and steadiness where the mind first attaches. The sequence is about the relation of attention to its object, not only the object's decreasing intensity. Verse 90 then compares absorption to a bee intent on nectar rather than the flower's scent. Several images carry a changing relation; no isolated label gives me that movement.\nThat is what interests me beyond a one-word definition of anāhata. A form can gather attention without remaining its loudest object. In a drawing, a repeated mark might prepare the reader to notice an interval after the mark disappears. That is a creative response to the reading: the composition could carry a consequence that a larger sacred emblem would merely announce.\nWhen the sound stops\nA story can make knowledge actionable through a changed relationship. A person might already know the relevant fact but be unable to act on it until trust, attention or their relation to another witness changes. That gives the reading a narrative question to work on without assigning a fixed fictional meaning to the text's symbol.\nConsider a neutral compositional study rather than a proposed scene. Two people have learnt to listen for the same tone, yet each has treated the other as an unreliable witness. The tone stops. What matters now is whether they can recognise each other's attention without the external cue that previously organised it. One might act, the other might refuse, and either choice can change their trust. Silence has acquired a consequence. Drawing a glowing symbol between them would be weaker if it supplied only an announcement that something sacred happened.\nThe visual work could preserve that consequence through position, interval and negative space. A repeated alignment can remain after its central mark has disappeared. The reader sees that a relation persists although the object used to announce it no longer does. A symbol's transformation has become something the composition can carry, rather than a definition it must display.\nMeaning moves with the encounter\nA symbol's function depends on its neighbours, on who meets it and on what the encounter changes. The Markandeya passages make the heart a setting for a divine arrival (12.10.10); the yoga passage follows inward sound towards absorption. Shared language can invite a comparison without establishing identical experiences or doctrines. The difference between their movements is part of their substance.\nAn invented relation can then change what someone believes, how they act and what the reader encounters. Those orders of a story give the symbol room to work over time. Its significance may emerge through a gesture recognised later, or through trust altered by a silence. The meaning belongs to the encounter, not to a label detached from everything that happens around it.\nIn the chapter IV text, the English paragraphs are numbered one lower than the Sanskrit verses cited here: 84–86 correspond to 83–85, 87–89 to 86–88, 90 to 89, and 101–102 to 100–101.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-vedic-world-inquiry","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-markandeya-encounters","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/"}}]}],"description":"A reading of adjacent Bhāgavata chapters, from recognition inside an immense world to the devotion its witness asks to keep.","text":"An immense revelation can seem like the end of a story. In the Bhāgavata, Markandeya's encounter with the cosmic child continues into another meeting, another setting and a request. Reading the adjacent chapters together lets the later response change what the earlier spectacle means.\nThe first article stays with the immense world, and the second follows what the witness does next. The order matters because seeing more does not settle what the witness wants, or how he relates to what he has seen.\nThe encounter also leaves me with a question about description: what happens when the world no longer fits the terms in which a witness understood it? Causality takes up that question in mathematical terms, asking which distinctions a description can express and when it needs to change. Here I am reading a theological encounter, with its own meaning and ending; the question can travel without turning the encounter into a mathematical claim.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-vedic-world-inquiry","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world","@type":"Article","name":"Markandeya and the size of a world","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-reading-a-tradition","@type":"Article","name":"What the witness asks for","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world","@type":"Article","name":"Markandeya and the size of a world","headline":"Markandeya and the size of a world","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["Markandeya","Bhagavata Purana 12.9","recognition and cosmic scale","world within the child","Markandeya's hermitage"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Markandeya and the size of a world","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Markandeya and the size of a world","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/"]},"description":"Inside the cosmic child, Markandeya recognises his hermitage. The familiar survives the impossible change of scale.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-03-04","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Markandeya and the size of a world\nThe detail that holds my attention is not just the size of the universe. It is that the familiar world is still there. In the Bhaktivedanta rendering of the Bhāgavata Purāṇa, 12.9.27, Markandeya is drawn into the child's body with an inhalation. What he encounters inside is a world he recognises. The narrative turns what seemed to contain everything into something contained, then lets recognition complicate the impossible change of scale.\nThe ordinary survives the extraordinary\nThe following verses, 12.9.28–29, do not remain at the level of abstract cosmic grandeur. The account includes rivers, settlements, occupations and the divisions of time. In 12.9.30, the witness recognises his own hermitage before the child's exhalation returns him to the waters.\nThat movement changes the frame without making the particulars irrelevant. Rivers, work and the divisions of time give the witness more than an abstract universe to contemplate. Recognition reaches as far as his own hermitage, something intimate within the immense account. My reading is that an altered perspective can make familiar life strange without making it disposable. The encounter's force comes through the persistence of particulars in a place where the witness did not expect them.\nThe witness gives the scale somewhere to land\nA diagram can show one container inside another. A narrative gives the change to someone who has to undergo it. Recognition, bewilderment and the return to a previous frame make cosmic scale a sequence of experiences. I can follow the encounter through a witness who cannot immediately reconcile what he sees, even when I cannot reconcile the frames myself. That is part of what draws me towards Markandeya in Sleepwalker's wider inquiry: his limits matter alongside what he witnesses. A world vast enough to disorient him still contains a place he knows, holding bewilderment and recognition together.\nAn image could preserve the relationship\nA visual response might hold one ordinary detail through a radical change of scale: a river, a place, a recognisable human silhouette. The large world needs the small witness, and the witness needs something recognisable within the large world. Keeping that relationship in the composition could make scale felt through the particulars that survive it.\nI would delay the hermitage rather than place it in the opening diagram. First the reader encounters the unexpected world; only then does a small familiar shape bring it close. The visual sequence can let recognition happen, instead of labelling a nested universe before the witness has found his place in it.\nA familiar rhythm and a familiar place can each give an immense frame a point of contact. The breath supplies an interval we can experience; the hermitage supplies a place the witness recognises. Neither small thing merely demonstrates how large the frame has become. Each preserves a point from which that frame can be experienced.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-markandeya-encounters","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/#article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-reading-a-tradition","@type":"Article","name":"What the witness asks for","headline":"What the witness asks for","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"keywords":["Markandeya","Bhagavata Purana 12.10","maya and knowledge","Shiva in the heart","Markandeya's boon","devotion"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What the witness asks for","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"The Vedic-world inquiry","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What the witness asks for","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/"]},"description":"The Bhāgavata continues beyond Markandeya's cosmic encounter into absorption and devotion, changing the question the spectacle seemed to answer.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-03-11","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What the witness asks for\nWhat would I ask for after seeing a world inside a child? Greater sight, perhaps, or an explanation of how the familiar hermitage could exist in that impossible setting. Markandeya's next encounter takes the question elsewhere. The witness has seen something immense; what he asks to keep is a relationship.\nWhat follows the extraordinary encounter?\nThe familiar image of Markandeya inside a child's breath can end with cosmic amazement. The Bhāgavata continues. After the encounter, 12.10.2 gives the sage a prayer in which māyā can appear in the guise of knowledge and bewilder even divine beings. In the Bhaktivedanta Book Trust translation, he responds by taking shelter in Hari. The narrative's answer is devotional, not a better diagram of the universe. That changes the question I bring back to the encounter: what does seeing something immense do to the witness's confidence in what he knows?\nA spectacular revelation often gives a fictional character new explanatory power. Here I find another possibility. The experience can alter the relation to explanation itself. If apparent knowledge can participate in bewilderment, another impressive account need not resolve the difficulty. That is my reading of the narrative turn, rather than an argument that studying the world is futile. The text's devotional response and my question about confidence meet without becoming the same statement.\nThe heart is another setting\nThe next encounter moves inward. In 12.10.9, Markandeya's absorption is so complete that he does not notice Shiva and Uma arriving. 12.10.10 describes Shiva entering the hidden space of his heart, comparing the entrance to air passing through an opening. The cosmographic witness is now the site of an encounter he did not perceive externally. The narrative can make an enormous world intimate without shrinking its significance.\nWhat attracts me is the movement between settings. The sage recognises his hermitage inside the cosmic child; later a divine visitor enters the sage's interior. A simple inside-and-outside diagram cannot carry the whole relation, because whose interior it is and how the encounter happens both change. The text makes the reader follow a person through those changes. That attention to encounter is richer than assigning each figure one permanent function.\nWhen a boon is offered, 12.10.34 has Markandeya ask for unwavering devotion to the Lord, the Lord's devotees and Shiva. The BBT commentary explicitly interprets Shiva's place through its Vaishnava theology. That interpretive setting matters. It is not a neutral legend in which familiar names can be freely substituted, nor a statement that every tradition arranges their relationships this way.\nThe sequence has moved twice: from the witness seeing an immense world to a visitor entering the witness, then from extraordinary perception to a request about devotion. The boon does not merely reward how much he has seen. It concerns the relation he wishes to keep. That makes the final request an essential part of my reading, rather than an epilogue after the interesting cosmic scene.\nRecognition does not make every account one biography\nThe short-life Markandeya, the encounter with death and Shiva, and the witness to dissolution invite comparison. They should not be joined merely because the name is familiar. Even within these two adjacent Bhāgavata chapters, a close reading discovers different relations: witness and world, knowledge and bewilderment, inner absorption and external arrival, gift and devotion. A comparison with another text needs the particular account it contributes, not a generic “Markandeya story” assembled from memory.\nThis is where the inquiry becomes personally useful. I begin with a name I recognise, follow a passage until it changes the question, and return to the name with less certainty but more substance. The world inside the child remains immense, but its witness no longer appears only as someone who saw it. His absorption and the devotion he requests give the encounter a life beyond its spectacle. Sometimes that reading changes the fiction. Sometimes it changes only the reader. Both are reasons to continue.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/reading-a-tradition/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-markandeya-encounters","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Through Markandeya's encounters","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/privacy/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-privacy","@type":"WebPage","name":"Privacy notice","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"description":"Website hosting, email contact, reading preferences and external reading assistance for RTEPL's editorial publications.","text":"Privacy notice\nRawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited (RTEPL) is responsible for deciding how personal data is used on rawthoughts.in, causality.in, sleepwalker.in and post-agi-economics.org, and on their subdomains, including the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in. RTEPL publishes and operates these websites. This notice explains the information handled when you read these websites or use their contact and reading tools.\nVisiting the website\nGitHub Pages hosts these publications. Serving a page involves connection information, including your IP address and the page requested. GitHub logs visitors’ IP addresses for security, including when a visitor is not signed in. GitHub handles that information under its privacy statement and may process it outside India.\nMintlify hosts the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in. Serving those pages involves the same connection information, which Mintlify handles under its privacy policy and may process outside India. Mintlify's own visitor telemetry is turned off for these sites. Mintlify also provides those two sites' search, page tools and display settings, under the same privacy policy; the in-browser search, assistant tools, display settings and passage links described below belong to the other websites.\nOn the other websites, search runs in your browser against each website's own index, so what you type is not sent to a search service. Your search is kept in the search page's address, so like any address it can reach GitHub with a page request, appear in your browser history and go with the address if you share it.\nIf your browser has an assistant that supports it, each page offers that assistant two read-only tools: the text of the page you are reading and the same in-browser search. They work on what the page already shows and send nothing to us; what the assistant does with what it reads follows its provider's terms.\nContacting us\nA contact link opens a new message in your own email application. The website does not receive or store the message as a form submission. If you choose to send it, your email service and the recipient's mail service handle the correspondence. Any professional work that follows has its own correspondence and agreement.\nFor privacy questions, a grievance or a request concerning your personal data, write to info@rtepl.co.in. Include enough information to identify the request, but do not send passwords or unnecessary identity documents. We respond within 30 days and may ask for proportionate verification before disclosing or changing personal data.\nReading preferences\nThe display settings remember your colour and motion choices in your browser on this device. You can change them in Settings or clear the website's stored data through your browser. These preferences are used to display the page; they stay in your browser and are not sent to us.\nAsking about a passage\nWhen you select text and choose a reading assistant, the link opens that provider with the selected passage, nearby context, the section and article titles, the publication's name, and the article and publication addresses. The passage is limited to 4,096 characters and the nearby context to 1,024. Selecting text alone prepares the link in your browser; choosing the provider sends it there.\nThe assistant is an external service. The request is included in its link, so it can also appear in your browser history. The provider's own terms, privacy policy and your account settings govern what it does with the request and any conversation that follows. Reading the article does not require using an assistant.\nHow long information is kept\nWe keep no record of visits on our own systems; GitHub and Mintlify keep their connection logs for the periods their policies set. Correspondence you send is kept for as long as the matter it concerns needs it and as the law requires.\nSecurity and incidents\nThese websites hold no accounts and receive no form submissions. If a personal-data breach affects you, we inform you and the authorities as applicable law requires.\nYour choices\nFollowing another external link takes you to that service under its own data practices.\nYou may ask us about your data, request correction or deletion, withdraw consent or raise a concern using the contact above. Requests are handled under applicable law; information needed to comply with a legal obligation or resolve a dispute may need to be kept. Withdrawing consent does not undo processing that has already occurred lawfully.\nChanges to the website's data practices will be reflected in this notice. A new optional use that requires consent will ask for that consent rather than treating continued reading as agreement.\nLast reviewed on 22 November 2026 by RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/privacy/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/search/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-search","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Search","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":15,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/about/","@type":"AboutPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/about/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-about","name":"About Sleepwalker"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-prompt-is-not-a-production-method","name":"An image is a beginning"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-essays","name":"Blog"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-creative-methods","name":"Creative methods"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/following-a-symbol-through-its-transformations/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-symbol-is-not-a-dictionary-entry","name":"Following a symbol through its transformations"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/","identifier":"journey:sleepwalker-from-frame-to-sequence","name":"From frame to sequence"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-continuity-is-more-than-a-seed","name":"Keeping a character through a sequence"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":8,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world","name":"Markandeya and the size of a world"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":9,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/privacy/","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/privacy/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-privacy","name":"Privacy notice"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":10,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-home","name":"Sleepwalker"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":11,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-terms","name":"Terms of use"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":12,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/","identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-vedic-world-inquiry","name":"The Vedic-world inquiry"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":13,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-information-architecture-for-a-story","name":"The several orders of a story"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":14,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-scale-is-not-a-measure-of-consciousness","name":"The world inside a breath"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":15,"item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/collections/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-collections","name":"Thoughts"}}]},"description":"Find pages, articles and collections from Sleepwalker.","text":"","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/search/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-terms","@type":"WebPage","name":"Terms of use","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"description":"Use and reuse of RTEPL's editorial publications and contact with the publisher.","text":"Terms of use\nRawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited (RTEPL) publishes and operates RawThoughts, Causality, Sleepwalker and Post-AGI Economics. These terms concern your use of rawthoughts.in, causality.in, sleepwalker.in and post-agi-economics.org, and of every subdomain of them, such as the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in.\nReading and reusing material\nUnless a page states its own licence, the content of these four publications, their text, images and data, is not openly licensed. You may read it, link to its pages and quote passages from it with attribution: name the author or publication and link to the page you quote. We permit that use, and it leaves untouched any wider right you have under fair dealing (section 52 of India's Copyright Act, 1957) or fair use elsewhere. The permission covers accurate quotation only; it does not cover misquoting the material or quoting it out of context so that it says something it does not. Reproducing the material without attribution, or presenting it as your own, is not permitted.\nTraining AI models on this material is not permitted, whether it is collected from these websites, their subdomains or any other copy, and training crawlers are refused. Search engines and answer systems that link to the pages and cite them remain welcome; only training is refused. This reservation is also our opt-out from text and data mining wherever the law provides one. Each website and subdomain carries it in its robots.txt and a machine-readable reservation wherever its host lets us publish them; where a host does not, this page still applies. These terms do not withdraw permissions granted by a licence or change a third party's rights.\nFor other copying, adaptation or redistribution, follow the licence attached to the page, asset or source, or ask us or the rights holder named on the item. Attribution alone does not replace permission.\nAuthors, contributors and third parties retain the rights attributed to them. Quotations, translations, images and software do not acquire a common licence merely by appearing on this website. A software repository's licence governs that software, not all the prose or images published here.\nThe scope of the publication\nThe publications cover applied ideas, economic inquiry, research and creative work. Read a research model with its stated assumptions and limits; its description does not guarantee how a deployed system will perform. Economic arguments and scenarios offer neither personal investment, tax or financial advice nor a transaction or guaranteed forecast. Interpretations of source texts, fiction and artistic experiments should be read in that form, rather than taken as medical guidance or scientific findings.\nConsulting work, software use, support and service levels follow their own agreements. Reading these pages or sending an enquiry does not enter you into one. Software, datasets, source texts, translations and contributed works keep their own licences and rights.\nWe aim to keep the material accurate and correct errors brought to our attention. Sources, methods and circumstances can change; a dated account concerns the setting it describes. You remain responsible for assessing whether material is suitable for your circumstances and for seeking qualified advice where needed.\nResponsible use\nDo not use the website to infringe another person's rights, expose confidential information, impersonate someone, interfere with its operation or attempt unauthorised access.\nExternal websites and services have their own terms. A reference is not an assurance of their accuracy, security or continued availability.\nAvailability and responsibility\nThe publication is provided as information. We do not promise uninterrupted access or that every example will suit a particular purpose. To the extent permitted by applicable law, we make no warranty of fitness for your use and are not responsible for loss arising from reliance on the material or interruption of access. Nothing here excludes a responsibility or remedy that the law does not allow us to exclude.\nPrivacy and contact\nThe privacy notice explains the information handled by this publication’s website and reading tools. A new use requiring consent will ask for it; continued reading is not agreement to that use.\nFor a correction, permission request or concern about these terms, write to info@rtepl.co.in. Please identify the page and the issue so we can respond.\nGoverning law\nIndian law governs these terms. Subject to mandatory law and any jurisdiction that cannot lawfully be excluded, disputes concerning use of the website fall within the jurisdiction of the competent courts in Lucknow. These terms do not replace a separate contract, licence or statutory right.\n","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/terms/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/oldest/","@type":"CollectionPage","creativeWorkStatus":"published","description":"Standalone essays on cosmic scale and the craft of making a story.","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-nocturne"}]},"identifier":"collection:sleepwalker-essays","inLanguage":"en","mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/#article","@type":"Article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-a-scale-is-not-a-measure-of-consciousness","name":"The world inside a breath","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/"},"position":1},{"@type":"ListItem","item":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/#article","@type":"Article","identifier":"content:sleepwalker-